From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Agent <claude-agent@invalid>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 14:54:31 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] backends/color: Take native SDR target primaries from the
 base EDID

The native SDR mode renders in the gamut of the base EDID color
characteristics but had no target gamut, so colors from wider sources
were clipped at the edges of the panel's gamut rather than compressed
into it.

Select the target primaries for every active output whose HDR is not
forced from the one source its mode allows, taken as a whole when it is
usable:

 - in BT.2100, the DisplayID 2 native chromaticities, wherever they are
   declared, with their existing validation. The base EDID color
   characteristics describe the default SDR colorimetry, which many HDR
   displays declare narrower than what they show in HDR, so they are no
   HDR target;
 - in the native SDR mode, the base EDID color characteristics, the
   gamut it encodes in, accepted as the mode accepts them before
   rendering with them, and, as published, within the chromaticity
   domain the DisplayID declaration is held to and with a usable
   geometry. Their tolerance is one 10-bit EDID step. Colors outside
   the encoding gamut clip whatever the target, so no other declaration
   is a target there.

In the native SDR mode the target then equals the encoding, and content
from wider encodings is compressed toward the panel's gamut. The
default SDR mode renders into an assumed sRGB encoding and keeps the
fallback. The caller states which source the output's mode allows, so
that the color device stays the one owner of the selection.

The native route fixture phase that leaves the route now also withdraws
the DisplayID chromaticities to reach the fallback, and checks that
leaving the route alone keeps both DisplayID targets.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DLZiLBYev72J1s4D37L7Bi
